INCANT'S ROADS TO A NODE DO NOT ALL PRESERVE IDENTITY

This is one issue wearing four masks. Run the incantation above and read the
four roads side by side. Only road 1 hands you the node you asked for.

WHAT IS WRONG

Road 2, the `<-` rebind, hands back a COPY. canonOf prints its pointer and the
copy's pointer and says DIFFERENT NODES. The copy's definingRule still resolves
to the original, so it looks right from every angle except one.

Road 3 reads through a capture first, deliberately, so it demonstrates the
parenT defect and not road 4's. Road 3, the parenT accessor, returns a WRAPPER whose group is the parent -- and
unWrap on that wrapper lands back on the CHILD it was applied to, not the
parent. Three spellings were tried on 2026-08-22 and none reached the parent,
which is why definingRule() could not be reconstructed from incant at all and
why canonOf had to be written as a C++ exposure.

Road 4, a chained subscript in a read position, returns garbage. In print
position it echoes tags (bear-trap 35). In ASSIGNMENT position it produced the
string xlInSet where the answer was 3. Measured while converting incant/frontier.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS

A copy answers every READ correctly. Same tag, same flags, same list, same
definingRule. It diverges only when you MUTATE it -- and then the write lands
on the copy, and every read-back off that copy CONFIRMS THE WRITE. The probe
reports success. The system never changed. There is no error, no crash, no
diagnostic, and the fixture goes green.

WHAT IT COST, and every one of these was reported as a finding before it was
caught -- one week, four wrong conclusions, all from the same root:

    "the catalog face and canon are DIFFERENT NODES"   -- copy vs canon
    "setParse does not move the action"                -- bound a copy
    "commissioning breaks setParse"                    -- bound a copy
    "the fork reads null, so binding is unreachable"   -- bound a copy

The last one sent a whole ruling (E) to the architect's desk asking which node
should carry a binding. The answer was: the ordinary one, and we had never
actually written to it.

THE FIX WANTED

A ruling first, then possibly nothing. Is copy-on-`<-` INTENDED semantics? It
is the architect's language and nobody should probe for intent. If intended,
this file becomes documentation plus a standing discipline. If unintended, it
is a defect whose blast radius is one measured week.

The parenT unwrap is separate and looks like a plain defect either way: an
accessor that cannot name a parent has no use.

NEXT: BEST GUESS

Two candidates, graded, and explicitly NOT a recommendation.

  1. COPY-ON-<- IS INTENDED (grade: likely). `<-` is documented as "rebinds a
     local's slot to a fresh node", and "fresh node" is exactly what was
     measured. If so the remedy is documentation and a discipline -- MUTATE
     through a direct subscript or an iterate cursor, READ through a capture --
     and this citizen promotes into the fleet as the regression test that the
     three roads keep behaving as documented.
     PROMOTED OR KILLED BY: the architect's word. Nothing to measure.

  2. THE parenT UNWRAP IS A PLAIN DEFECT (grade: likely, independent of 1).
     opDot case 2 sets product.group = target.parent, and unWrap walks
     `while isGROUP grup = group` -- whose bare-name resolution is ambiguous
     enough that it may be walking the wrong operand. That is a reading, not a
     diagnosis.
     PROMOTED OR KILLED BY: reading unWrap's generated code in GroupRules.mm and
     checking which object each bare name resolved to. One grep, not a build.

WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS FILE GO QUIET: road 2 printing SAME NODE, road 3 printing
Braced, and road 4's first read printing 3. Any of the three can move
independently.

⚠ STANDING DISCIPLINE, effective regardless of the ruling: IDENTITY QUESTIONS
ARE ASKED IN POINTERS, THROUGH canonOf. A resolver that reports names cannot
answer a question about identity -- two faces of one rule share a tag by
construction, and that is precisely how this hid for a week.
